title Reading one rule is not reading the rules
prompt Keaton -- land 689; does the v2/v3 naming follow stamp-and-name; does that not follow our gratitude on chronological versioning; mark this a red, tighten the rules, audit; prepare the watcher and a handoff
obs %689 CLOSED, and the road was short because %680 had already built it. An insert now carries the kept line it comes AFTER, takes that line's run and order key, and its fresh position sorts it just past the anchor -- a line placed between two others without a denser number than the field holds.
obs THE CLAIM THAT RECORDED THE OLD LIMIT FLIPPED, and its own elder text had promised it would: asserting the true shape is what turns a later widening of Diff into a visible change. It widened; the change was visible.
obs I RULED A VERSION NUMBER LAWFUL FROM THE WRONG LAW. Asked whether v2/v3 follows stamp-and-name, I reasoned from the census exemption and wrote that ruling INTO the law. context/specs/rye-versioning-style.md had settled it the other way -- versions here are chronological, refusing semantic versioning by name and citing Hickey's Spec-ulation, a talk this tree keeps in its gratitude.
obs KEATON CAUGHT IT TWICE: first that the naming might not follow the rule, then that our own gratitude reading already answered it. I had checked stamp-and-name and the Lexicon and never the spec that owns the subject.
obs THE LESSON IS ABOUT READING RATHER THAN ABOUT VERSIONS. A law can be read correctly and still be the wrong law. Before ruling a number lawful, ask whether a spec already rules on that KIND of number.
obs AUDITED THE CLASS RATHER THAN THE INSTANCE: 111 counted version strings across 42 record families stand in authored Rye, and only mantra-weave is moved. The remaining 41 write bytes a store already carries, so they want Keaton's word.
obs Elder headers keep their names forever, because a version sits in content-addressed bytes and renaming it would change the identity of every object ever written. Only what the tree WRITES moved.
